## Image Caching Discussion

```
got it, you might be doing more work than you have to

1:45
If wordpress tells the image component that the TTL is 60s (via Cache-Control):
It'll cache at the edge for 60s
Calls after 60s should respect the modified-at and fall back to cache. So you're getting the HEAD of the file, but not streaming the whole damn thing again (or paying for the transform).
Probably no need to double cache on the fetch side.
1:46
The "manual" caching that you're doing is likely pretty effective, but you're just re-doing the work of that image component (if I'm reading these docs correctly)
1:46
If you didn't control that backend (e.g. wordpress), your cache strategy would be the best option
1:47
We can test and measure of course, but that's my read
1:49
You also may be getting more cache invalidations because you're not normalizing the headers
1:50
the fetch strategy does make sense for json blobs and such that are expensive
