## Accessing Value for Monitoring

```
It's really a question for the business. If you think you already have a solid mechanism for detecting impactful outages, I would continue using that. The goal with any tool like this should be to decrease your "mean time to resolution" (MTR). If changes are only done during the hours that people are paying attention, then most regressions will happen while people are watching.
So when evaluating what the thresholds should be here, it should be somewhere around "your most online person" on your staff. I feel like at any given company there's at least one or two stakeholders who will KNOW when somethings is broken. Luckily my example is portable with this crowd: Theron.
When we (read: I) broke the feed, Theron usually noticed before anyone else internal or externally would notice because his feed was more or less a real-time view into the inventory on reverb.com. If Theron's feed was broken, the feed was likely broken at least in some subtle way. So the goal was always: "can we notice something is wrong in an automated fashion that is as effective as Theron without needing to pay for Theron"
```

